{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>On the day after the Sabbath the priest shall wave it.</b> [This means] on the day after the first festival day of Passover, for if you say [it means] the Sabbath of creation, you would not know which one — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 23:11). And in truth this is the greatest of the proofs in the Gemara, for what reason would Scripture say: when you come into the land and you reap its harvest, you shall bring the omer on the day after the Sabbath of creation? For it could be understood to mean that at whatever time of the year we come into the land and reap its harvest, the priest shall wave the omer on the day after the first Sabbath that occurs after our entry into the land, and behold the festival of Shavuot would have no fixed time counted from it. And furthermore, on this interpretation we would not know in the years that follow when to begin counting — [it would be] only from the day one begins to sickle the standing grain, whenever we please, and these are words of emptiness. But if \"on the day after the Sabbath\" means the festival day according to the tradition of our Sages, the matter comes out properly, for Scripture first commanded (Leviticus 23:6–8) that in the first month we observe the Festival of Unleavened Bread for seven days, and on the first day a Sabbath-rest and on the seventh day a Sabbath-rest, and no manner of laborious work shall be done, and afterward it says (Leviticus 23:9–11) that when we come into the land we shall bring, on the day after this Sabbath — which is mentioned in connection with the omer of waving — [the omer]; and this is the first Sabbath mentioned here, and it teaches that the omer rite was not practiced in the wilderness or outside the land. But \"on the day after the seventh Sabbath\" (Leviticus 23:16) and \"seven Sabbaths\" (Leviticus 23:15) cannot be interpreted as [referring to] a festival day; rather, Onkelos translates them with the language of \"week,\" and if so there would be two meanings in a single verse. And the commentators said that this is a figure of speech, like \"riding on thirty donkeys, and thirty donkeys for them\" (Judges 10:4), and in another place (Deuteronomy 16:9) it specifies \"seven weeks,\" and likewise \"those who come on the Sabbath with those who go out on the Sabbath\" (II Kings 11:9) — [meaning] a week, and likewise \"when will the new moon pass so that we may sell grain, and the Sabbath, that we may open the wheat\" (Amos 8:5), for since in every seven days there is one Sabbath, and the reckoning of days proceeds from it, a single week is called one Sabbath — one שַׁבָּת, and this is a well-known and familiar expression in the words of our Sages, who say: twice every Sabbath the court sits in the cities (Ketuvot 2a). And it is possible that every \"הַשַּׁבָּת\" mentioned in this passage means \"week,\" and the sense of \"on the day after the Sabbath\" would be to say that on the day of the waving of the omer one begins to count the week in its numbering; if so, that day would be the day after the week that has passed, as though it said: the day of waving shall be after a week of the elapsed days, and the first of the weeks that one counts until he completes seven Sabbaths. And because Scripture mentioned (Leviticus 23:6) \"on the fifteenth day,\" it says that one waves the omer on the day after the aforementioned week and counts from it seven Sabbaths. And one cannot err to [the fourteenth of] the day of the fourteenth, for it mentioned only \"between the evenings\" (Leviticus 23:5) of it. And the reason for \"they shall be complete\" (Leviticus 23:15) is that the Sabbath together with the six days is called a complete שַׁבָּת. And the meaning of \"מִמָּחֳרַת הַשַּׁבָּת\" is like בְּמָחֳרַת הַשַּׁבָּת (on the morrow of the Sabbath), and likewise \"until the day after the seventh Sabbath you shall count\" (Leviticus 23:16), and likewise \"on the day of your sacrifice it shall be eaten, and on the day after\" (Leviticus 19:6). And they said that the letter bet does not come with the word מָחָר or מָחֳרָת, [as in] \"and it was on the next day, and Moses sat to judge the people\" (Exodus 18:13), \"and YHWH did this thing on the next day\" (Exodus 9:6), and so all of them."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:58:34.277227228Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}